The return value is a numeric \strong{matrix} with \code{nrow(newdata)} rows and
\code{P * K} columns, where \code{P = max(n_param, 1)} and \code{K = max(n_innov, 1)}.
Column \code{(j - 1) * K + k} holds parameter draw \code{j}, innovation draw \code{k}.
Always a matrix, even at 1 x 1 (wrap with \code{as.vector()} if needed).
\itemize{
\item \code{n_param = 0}: condition on the point estimates (no parameter
uncertainty), \code{P = 1}. \code{n_param >= 1}: that many independent parameter
draws.
\item \code{n_innov = 0}: return the conditional mean per parameter draw (this
subsumes \code{predict(..., what = "expectation")}), \code{K = 1}. \code{n_innov >= 1}:
independent innovations per row within each parameter block.
\item \code{param_scope} (linear, glm, and glmmTMB methods only): \code{"draw"} (default)
uses one parameter deviate per column block, shared across rows —
coherent replicates for analysis. \code{"row"} uses an independent parameter
deviate per row — the simulation engine's row-expansion convention where
each grid row is its own sim world; the engine's \verb{predict.*} calls use
this scope so ensemble statistics are unchanged.
}
